      <h2 id="related">Where to go next</h2>
      <p>The document that names these posts and defines their powers is the constitution, and most clubs' are older than they think: <a href="/guides/badminton-club-constitution">writing a badminton club constitution</a> covers which template to start from and which clauses in it are wrong.</p>
      <p>The treasurer's actual job is the two articles next door: <a href="/guides/setting-club-subs">setting club subs</a> covers what to charge, and <a href="/guides/collecting-club-subs">collecting club subs</a> covers getting paid without losing your evenings.</p>
      <p>And if you are assembling a committee because you are starting from scratch, <a href="/guides/how-to-start-a-badminton-club">how to start a badminton club</a> covers the rest of it.</p>

      <div style="margin-top:34px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 8px">How this guide was put together</h4>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:0">ePegboard builds club-night software. We are not lawyers and we have never sat on a badminton club committee. Badminton England's requirements here were read from their own pages on 26 August 2026, including their club committee guidance, the Club Welfare Officer role description and the club training matrix. The club examples come from nine published badminton club constitutions read the same day.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:12px 0 0">Three limits worth stating. Nine constitutions found through search is a convenience sample, not a picture of the sector. Nobody publishes how many affiliated clubs have appointed a trained welfare officer, so we have given no compliance figure and would treat any you are shown with suspicion. And the liability hierarchy described above comes from tax regulations covering specific levies; the principle is sound and its precise reach across all club debts is not something we could establish.</p>
